#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538044} :activity/id #uuid "86690471-d357-5c6d-a479-0d9d6dca4cdc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049557 :topic/id #uuid "fee59c68-0ad2-5141-b297-31cf8632cc46" :topic/math-academy-id 2167} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538042 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269135 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32b3d1a3-e2c5-53af-933b-517ec9a0d72b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266589} {:answer/id #uuid "5c45d0cb-d619-548e-8823-23b2000f9c8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266959} {:answer/id #uuid "5f359553-9efb-5c8e-94ff-571d277b5bfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266983} {:answer/id #uuid "d1706cd6-61bf-52a6-816c-b55ddc048d76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268018} {:answer/id #uuid "e5d0f2d7-43a5-560a-91a8-6d0fe37c6f97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268179}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266983} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ca2f21a-e889-5f63-95ad-6473117737ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a9599aec-b36e-5461-9425-547d03dffdb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-136875" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=4 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}20 & a & 10 \\\\ c & 80 & -20 \\\\ 10 & -20 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}22 \\\\ b \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations and its corresponding normal equation shown above. What is the value of $a-b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe normal equation corresponding to $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -8 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -8 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}20 & -40 & 10 \\\\ -40 & 80 & -20 \\\\ 10 & -20 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}22 \\\\ -44 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\na-b+c=(-40)-(-44)+(-40)=-36.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a688d4f0-219c-53ce-8859-4cf2cfa1b6e7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538049}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538043 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134374 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "195f5438-dbcb-5fdf-b558-bce11648bd92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324187} {:answer/id #uuid "34da3d3b-8b0d-5731-9512-7880150858c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324401} {:answer/id #uuid "acc58718-2cff-5c4d-a0a5-bc1a9d33067c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325439} {:answer/id #uuid "cd585b41-d5de-5636-9761-c198aae3b03f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325751} {:answer/id #uuid "edef752f-66e7-582a-9d21-188b3b7ac00b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326067}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325751} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c9ed528-04bf-5ec5-8933-3ee5c01fd349" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "00e4806c-b4ce-579d-8d0e-03b84320da38" :question/math-academy-id "q-41919" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $30a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}30 & -30 \\\\ -30 & 30\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}30 & -30 & 1 \\\\ -30 & 30 & -1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}30 & -30 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{30}+{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{30}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{30} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n30a+\\frac{b}{c}=30\\cdot \\frac{1}{30}+\\frac{1}{1}=2.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a9d99bb3-bbe7-5cd2-986b-c2d9fa71e9d6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538042}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538044 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c20af83-4b36-5128-89ee-a2b0f277470a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324458} {:answer/id #uuid "58d3b34f-653e-5908-8110-49345a2e51c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324708} {:answer/id #uuid "5c3eb528-5cf9-526f-857f-315ec81f0eb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324735} {:answer/id #uuid "b0bbe617-b13b-516f-806c-a9356a26279f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325479} {:answer/id #uuid "c5751e84-51f5-5505-bf51-a2e74963787c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325674}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325479} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac5ff18b-ee0a-5c8e-a1ac-a863dbac8c0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "85d7c223-d931-53ec-98fb-80a06e5b2764" :question/math-academy-id "q-41904" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $15a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}40 & -20 \\\\ -20 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -5 & 1 \\\\ -10 & 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n15a+\\frac{b}{c}=15\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{1}=2.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "aa620879-5cfd-5e7d-a99d-239929c57208" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538043}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538049 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424019 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06a52bea-6ee1-5ba3-91a9-fa2c837b2777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424006} {:answer/id #uuid "1c6dd795-91e5-55e1-984a-5549435634d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424007} {:answer/id #uuid "8612d6bf-1d46-5e5b-bb7f-ca4d007e7546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424011} {:answer/id #uuid "a4dbc700-783c-5f48-a597-56c6ebf71f61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424013} {:answer/id #uuid "d4b6a212-c60d-5b71-a8fe-b61548150715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424015}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424015} :answer-field/id #uuid "389ee680-9312-52e5-a68e-aecf86a358ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4cb13173-852b-58a2-8c1f-3d6511a05fac" :question/math-academy-id "q-136920" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}20 & 10 & a \\\\ 10 & 5 & 5 \\\\ c & 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations and its corresponding normal equation shown above. What is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe normal equation corresponding to $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}20 & 10 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 10 & 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=10+4+10=24.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ba011249-f521-57db-a1bf-2b091aa5de6e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538056}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538056 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367436 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c464ae52-c792-5966-9d49-a2e4829b9b98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367438} {:answer/id #uuid "60181620-1848-5691-9b01-0836bdae2569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367439} {:answer/id #uuid "1ffce98a-3785-54ef-a67a-a79256e5e528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367440} {:answer/id #uuid "61d68627-501b-5d8d-a22c-84fdf7f0c3a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367441} {:answer/id #uuid "5a336a5d-1d0b-5110-8865-6d48c421f50f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367442}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367440} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac10a081-d7a4-5b23-9a50-e6500fcd48c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367437}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8e0db89b-f0c6-50f0-a4df-0e579944e4eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-137033" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\\,+\\,{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a-4b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 12 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 12 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 3 & -2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 12 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-6{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second row, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(-\\frac{3}{2})+2{x}_{3}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-1-2{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1-2{x}_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na-4b+c=-2-4\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{2})+0=4.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c9b55ea0-c7ec-5e84-a315-6b02347bb893"}] :activity/title "The Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System (With Collinearity)" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537213}]}
